{"command": "cd /testbed && cat specs/001-transactions/contracts/batch-writer.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "# Contract: tinydb.journal.BatchWriter\n\nFeature: `specs/001-transactions/spec.md` | Date: 2026-10-17\n\nThis is the only new public surface for the feature. Everything else is\nunchanged (Constitution I).\n\n## Module\n\n`tinydb/journal.py`, exporting `BatchWriter`.\n\n## Constructor\n\n```python\nBatchWriter(db: TinyDB) -> BatchWriter\n```\n\n- Binds the writer to the supplied `TinyDB` instance and its default table\n  (`db.table(db.default_table_name)`).\n- Performs no writes and no schema changes; construction is side-effect free.\n- Does not alter `db`'s ordinary APIs (reads/writes on `db` or any table handle\n  behave exactly as before).\n\n## Method: apply\n\n```python\napply(operations: list[dict]) -> list[int]\n```\n\n### Inputs\n\n- `operations`: a list; each element MUST be a dict of exactly one of the two\n  valid shapes (see data-model.md): `{'op': 'insert', 'document': dict}` or\n  `{'op': 'remove', 'doc_id': int > 0}`. Extra keys are ignored (A5).\n\n### Outputs\n\n- On success: a plain `list[int]` containing the IDs of the inserted documents,\n  in insertion order. Removes contribute nothing. Empty input -> `[]`.\n\n### Errors\n\n- `ValueError` for any of:\n  - `operations` is not a list, or an element is not a dict;\n  - `op` missing or not in `{'insert', 'remove'}`;\n  - insert: `document` missing or not a `Mapping`;\n  - remove: `doc_id` missing, not an `int` (bool excluded), or `<= 0`;\n  - remove: `doc_id` absent from the table when the operation's turn arrives\n    (R02: missing-ID remove is an error).\n- On `ValueError` the default table is restored to its complete pre-batch state\n  (contents, IDs, order) and the error propagates to the caller.\n\n### Atomicity and visibility\n\n- The batch is all-or-nothing: either every operation took effect (one storage\n  write) or the pre-batch state is fully restored (one storage write).\n- Previously acquired `db.table('_default')` handles reflect both outcomes on\n  their next read without re-acquisition.\n- Only the default table is modified; other tables in the storage are carried\n  through unchanged.\n\n### Aliasing\n\n- Stored documents are deep copies of the caller's input; mutating a caller\n  document or operation after `apply` returns cannot mutate stored data (R03).\n- The return value contains IDs only.\n\n## Non-goals (out of contract)\n\n- No multi-table batches, no update op, no persistence format change, no\n  threading/locking guarantees, no context-manager or async API.\n"}